// Question pool 13: serverless, messaging and containers. Exam level. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"q13-001", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A logistics company's Lambda function is invoked asynchronously by Amazon S3 event notifications whenever a carrier uploads a manifest file. For each file the function makes one call to a partner's tracking API, and the partner rejects requests when the company has more than 50 calls in progress at once. During the nightly upload peak the function scales to several hundred concurrent executions and most calls are rejected. Every manifest must still be processed within a few hours, and the company does not want to add components. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure provisioned concurrency of 50 on an alias of the function.",
    "Set the function's asynchronous retry attempts to 0 and its maximum event age to 60 seconds.",
    "Configure reserved concurrency of 50 on the function.",
    "Put an API Gateway REST API with a usage plan of 50 requests per second in front of the function."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Provisioned concurrency keeps 50 execution environments initialized to avoid cold starts. When they are busy, the function goes on scaling with unreserved concurrency, so nothing caps it at 50.",
    "These settings decide how often and for how long Lambda retries an asynchronous event. They do not limit how many executions run at once, and a 60-second age would discard manifests that wait during the peak.",
    "Reserved concurrency is both the minimum and the maximum for a function, so at most 50 executions call the partner at once. Throttled asynchronous events go back to Lambda's queue and are retried for up to 6 hours by default, so the manifests are processed later instead of being lost.",
    "S3 event notifications invoke the function directly, so an API would not be in the path, and it is an extra component. A rate per second is also not a limit on the number of calls in progress."],
 ref:[], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q13-002", day:13, dom:1, tags:["Lambda","Internet & NAT gateways"],
 q:"A Lambda function is attached to two private subnets of a VPC so that it can query an Amazon RDS database. A new feature requires the function to call a payment provider's public HTTPS API as well, and the provider accepts requests only from IP addresses that the company registers in advance. Calls to the provider currently time out. The database must stay unreachable from the internet. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a NAT gateway with an Elastic IP address in a public subnet, route the private subnets' internet-bound traffic to it, and register that address.",
    "Move the function to public subnets of the VPC, turn on auto-assign public IPv4 addresses for those subnets, and register the addresses.",
    "Remove the VPC configuration from the function so that it runs in the Lambda-managed network, which has internet access by default.",
    "Attach an internet gateway to the VPC, add a default route to it in the private subnets' route table, and register the function's address."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A function attached to a VPC reaches the internet only through the VPC. A public NAT gateway sits in a public subnet and must have an Elastic IP address, so every outbound call leaves from one fixed address that the provider can register, and nothing can connect inward.",
    "Connecting a function to a public subnet gives it neither internet access nor a public IP address, so the calls would still time out and there would be no address to register.",
    "Outside the VPC the function has internet access, but it can no longer reach the database in the private subnets, and its outbound address is not one the company controls.",
    "A default route to an internet gateway turns the subnets into public subnets, which is exactly where a function gets no internet access and no public IP address. It also weakens the isolation of the database subnets."],
 ref:[], doc:"nat"},

{id:"q13-003", day:13, dom:4, tags:["Lambda","SQS"],
 q:"An IoT company's Lambda function consumes an Amazon SQS standard queue that receives about 40 small meter readings per second around the clock. The event source mapping has no batching window, and monitoring shows that most invocations carry only one or two messages, so the function is invoked millions of times a day for a few milliseconds of work each. The function already loops over every record in the event. The readings feed an hourly report, so a delay of up to five minutes is acceptable. The company wants to lower the Lambda cost without changing the function's logic. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Configure provisioned concurrency on the function so that warm environments handle the readings.",
    "Turn on partial batch responses (<code>ReportBatchItemFailures</code>) for the event source mapping.",
    "Replace the event source mapping with an EventBridge Scheduler schedule that invokes the function every five minutes.",
    "Increase the event source mapping's batch size and set a batching window of up to five minutes."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Provisioned concurrency removes cold starts and adds a charge for the time it is configured. The function would still be invoked once for every one or two messages.",
    "Partial batch responses return only the failed messages of a batch to the queue. They change error handling, not the number of invocations.",
    "A function invoked by a schedule receives no messages; it would have to poll and delete them itself, which is a change to its logic.",
    "With a batching window Lambda keeps collecting messages for up to 300 seconds, or until the batch size or payload limit is reached, before it invokes the function. A standard queue allows batches of up to 10,000 messages, so the same readings are processed in far fewer invocations."],
 ref:[], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q13-004", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A trading firm publishes price updates to an Amazon SNS topic that invokes a Lambda function asynchronously; the function writes each price to a cache used by a web application. After an incident in which the function was throttled for two hours, Lambda delivered the waiting updates much later and old prices overwrote current ones. The firm decides that an update that has waited more than 5 minutes must never be applied, and that a record of every update dropped for this reason must be kept for an audit. No code changes are planned. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Set the function's maximum event age for asynchronous invocations to 5 minutes.",
    "Set the function's retry attempts for asynchronous invocations to 0.",
    "Add an on-failure destination for asynchronous invocations that sends records to an SQS queue.",
    "Shorten the delivery retry policy of the function's SNS subscription to 5 minutes.",
    "Configure reserved concurrency on the function so that it has capacity of its own."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Lambda keeps an asynchronous event in its internal queue for up to 6 hours by default. The maximum event age shortens that, so an update that has waited longer than 5 minutes is discarded instead of being run.",
    "Retry attempts count the retries after the function returns an error. A throttled event is put back in the queue and tried again until it reaches the maximum event age, whatever this setting says.",
    "An on-failure destination receives an invocation record when an event fails all attempts or exceeds the maximum age, so every discarded update ends up in the queue for the audit without any code.",
    "The SNS delivery policy covers retries while SNS cannot hand a message to the endpoint. Here Lambda has already accepted each event; the waiting happens in Lambda's own queue.",
    "Reserved concurrency makes throttling by other functions less likely, but it neither stops a late update from being applied nor records anything."],
 ref:[], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q13-005", day:13, dom:3, tags:["Lambda","S3 features"],
 q:"A mobile app lets inspectors upload site videos of up to 2 GB. Uploads go to an Amazon API Gateway REST API that passes the file to a Lambda function, which stores it in an S3 bucket and records metadata in DynamoDB. Small test files work, but real videos are rejected before the function runs. The company wants uploads of every expected size to succeed while the bucket stays private and the app never holds AWS credentials. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Increase the function's memory to 10,240 MB and its timeout to 15 minutes so that it can receive the whole video.",
    "Have the function return an S3 presigned URL, let the app upload the video straight to the bucket, and record metadata from an S3 event.",
    "Replace the REST API with an HTTP API, which accepts larger request bodies for Lambda integrations.",
    "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket and keep sending the uploads through the API and the function."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Memory and timeout do not change payload limits: API Gateway accepts a payload of at most 10 MB, and a synchronous Lambda invocation at most 6 MB.",
    "A presigned URL lets the app upload one object without AWS credentials, with the permissions of the function that created the URL, and a single PUT takes an object of up to 5 GB. The video never passes through API Gateway or Lambda, and an S3 event notification starts the metadata step.",
    "HTTP APIs have the same 10 MB payload limit, and the function behind them still takes at most 6 MB per synchronous invocation.",
    "The upload would still travel through API Gateway and Lambda, whose payload limits are far below 2 GB; how the data reaches S3 afterwards makes no difference."],
 ref:[], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q13-006", day:13, dom:3, tags:["Lambda","Containers"],
 q:"A genomics startup runs an analysis step as a Lambda function packaged as a container image. New sample types need about 48 GB of memory and up to 40 minutes per run. Runs are started a few times a day by an AWS Step Functions workflow, and no compute should exist between runs. The team has no capacity to patch or scale servers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Raise the function's memory to its maximum and split each run into three invocations that the workflow chains.",
    "Run the image on an ECS cluster of memory-optimized EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group with a minimum of one.",
    "Configure provisioned concurrency on the function for the hours in which samples usually arrive.",
    "Run the image as an Amazon ECS task on AWS Fargate that the workflow starts for each sample."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A function can have at most 10,240 MB of memory, and an invocation of a standard function ends after 15 minutes. Chaining invocations does not give any one of them the 48 GB the analysis needs.",
    "This fits the memory and run time, but an instance keeps running between runs and the team would have to patch and scale the instances itself.",
    "Provisioned concurrency keeps environments initialized. It changes neither the memory limit nor the 15-minute limit, and it is charged while configured.",
    "A Fargate task can have up to 16 vCPU and 120 GB of memory and has no 15-minute limit. Step Functions can run the task and wait for it, there are no servers to manage, and Fargate resources are used only from image pull until the task ends."],
 ref:["d1c8"], doc:"fargate"},

{id:"q13-007", day:13, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway"],
 q:"A SaaS company is designing a public API on Amazon API Gateway with Lambda integrations. Each customer must receive its own API key with a monthly request quota, malformed request bodies must be rejected before any Lambda function is invoked, and the security team requires an AWS WAF web ACL on the API. A developer proposes an HTTP API because it costs less per request. The company wants to meet the requirements with the LEAST custom code. Which design should a solutions architect choose?",
 o:["An HTTP API with a JWT authorizer, with one route per customer so that requests can be counted and limited.",
    "An HTTP API behind Amazon CloudFront with the web ACL, and a Lambda authorizer that validates bodies and counts requests.",
    "A REST API with usage plans and API keys, request validation, and the web ACL associated with its stage.",
    "A REST API with a Lambda authorizer that checks each customer's key and quota in a DynamoDB table."],
 a:[2],
 x:["HTTP APIs are built with fewer features so that they can cost less. They have no API keys, per-client quotas, request validation or AWS WAF integration, and a route per customer provides none of them.",
    "CloudFront can carry the web ACL, but validation and quotas would be code that the company writes and maintains in the authorizer. A REST API provides both as configuration.",
    "REST APIs support API keys with usage plans (per-client throttling and quotas), request validation before the integration is called, and AWS WAF. HTTP APIs support none of the three.",
    "A REST API is the right type, but keys and quotas are built into usage plans. An authorizer with a table is custom code for the same result, and it leaves validation and the web ACL unaddressed."],
 ref:[], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q13-008", day:13, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway"],
 q:"A travel company exposes a REST API on API Gateway that anyone can call without an API key. One method, <code>POST /quotes</code>, calls a legacy pricing engine that fails above 20 requests per second, while the other 30 methods are backed by DynamoDB and routinely serve thousands of requests per second. During promotions the pricing engine is overwhelmed and quote requests time out. The company wants excess quote requests to be rejected quickly with an HTTP 429 response without limiting the other methods. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Override the stage's throttling for the <code>POST /quotes</code> method with a rate of 20 requests per second.",
    "Set the default throttling rate of the whole stage to 20 requests per second.",
    "Create a usage plan with a rate of 20 requests per second and require an API key on every method.",
    "Enable API caching on the stage with a TTL of 300 seconds."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Stage throttling is applied per method, and one method can be given settings different from the rest. Requests above the target are answered with 429 Too Many Requests before they reach the engine. API Gateway applies throttles on a best-effort basis, so the value is a target, not a hard ceiling.",
    "The stage default applies to every method, so the 30 DynamoDB-backed methods would be throttled at 20 requests per second as well.",
    "Usage plan limits apply to each client that presents an API key. The API is called without keys today, and a limit of 20 per client still lets many clients together overload the engine.",
    "Caching is turned on only for GET methods by default and answers repeated identical requests. It does not cap the rate of quote requests that reach the engine."],
 ref:[], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q13-009", day:13, dom:3, tags:["API Gateway","Caching"],
 q:"A retailer serves its product catalogue through a REST API on API Gateway with stage caching enabled and a TTL of 3600 seconds, which removed almost all load from the Lambda functions behind it. Prices change once a night when a batch job finishes loading a new price list. The business now requires that the API returns no old price after the job has finished, and it wants to keep the benefit of the cache for the rest of the day. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Reduce the cache TTL of the stage to 0 seconds.",
    "Ask all API clients to send a <code>Cache-Control: max-age=0</code> header after the nightly job.",
    "Disable caching and configure provisioned concurrency on the Lambda functions.",
    "Have the batch job flush the stage cache as its last step."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A TTL of 0 means that caching is disabled, so every request would reach the functions again and the benefit is lost.",
    "That header invalidates one cache entry per request and works only for clients that are authorized to invalidate the cache. It depends on every client changing its behaviour.",
    "This gives up the cache. Provisioned concurrency removes cold starts but does not reduce the number of invocations.",
    "Flushing the stage cache makes API Gateway fetch fresh responses from the integration and build the cache up again, so one call at the end of the job is enough and the one-hour TTL stays in place."],
 ref:[], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q13-010", day:13, dom:1, tags:["API Gateway","IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A company's order service exposes an internal REST API on API Gateway in account A. The only callers are batch jobs on EC2 instances and Lambda functions in account B, all of which run with IAM roles whose policies already allow <code>execute-api:Invoke</code> on the API. The security team wants each request authenticated with the caller's existing AWS credentials, no additional passwords, keys or tokens to store or rotate, and access limited to two named roles from account B. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Set the methods' authorization type to IAM and have the callers sign requests with Signature Version 4.",
    "Create a Cognito user pool with one user per job and add a Cognito authorizer to the methods.",
    "Attach a resource policy to the API that allows only the two roles from account B to invoke it.",
    "Issue an API key to each job through a usage plan and require the key on every method.",
    "Add a Lambda authorizer that compares a header with a shared secret held in AWS Secrets Manager."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["With IAM authorization the callers sign each request with the temporary credentials of the role they already have, so nothing new has to be stored or rotated.",
    "User pool users bring passwords and tokens that every job would have to keep and renew, which is what the security team wants to avoid.",
    "For a caller in another account, both the caller's IAM policy and the API's resource policy must allow the call. The resource policy is where account A names the two roles and nobody else.",
    "AWS says not to use API keys for authentication or authorization; they identify clients for usage plans. They are also another secret to store.",
    "This is code to maintain and a shared secret to rotate, and it does not use the callers' AWS credentials."],
 ref:[], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q13-011", day:13, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway"],
 q:"A company runs its order microservices on Amazon ECS behind an internal Application Load Balancer in private subnets. A new mobile app must reach these services through a public Amazon API Gateway API that handles authentication and throttling. The security team insists that the load balancer stays internal and that no service receives a public IP address. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Change the load balancer's scheme to internet-facing and allow only HTTPS from the internet in its security group.",
    "Create a VPC link and configure the API's integrations as private integrations that target the internal load balancer.",
    "Change the API's endpoint type to private and create an interface VPC endpoint for <code>execute-api</code> in the VPC.",
    "Create a Lambda function in the private subnets for each route that forwards the request to the load balancer."],
 a:[1],
 x:["An internet-facing load balancer is what the security team has ruled out.",
    "A private integration exposes HTTP resources inside a VPC to clients outside it. The VPC link gives API Gateway network interfaces in the VPC, so the public API reaches the internal load balancer and nothing in the VPC becomes public.",
    "A private API can be called only from inside a VPC through an interface endpoint, so the mobile app could no longer reach it. The endpoint type decides who can call the API, not how the API reaches its backend.",
    "This works, but every route needs a function that the team writes, runs and pays for. A VPC link does the same without code."],
 ref:[], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q13-012", day:13, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway","Step Functions"],
 q:"A tax-software company offers a \"generate annual report\" function through an API Gateway REST API backed by a Lambda function. For large customers the report takes 20 to 40 minutes to build in several steps, so their requests fail with a timeout while small customers succeed. Customers use the API from unreliable mobile networks, a started report must be completed even if a step fails temporarily, and each customer must be able to see whether its report is still running. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Ask AWS to raise the API's integration timeout to 40 minutes, and set the Lambda function's timeout to the same value.",
    "Move the API to an HTTP API, whose integrations may run for as long as the backend needs.",
    "Have the API start a Step Functions Standard workflow and return its execution ID, and add a method that returns its status.",
    "Move the API to a WebSocket API and keep each customer's connection open until the report has been built."],
 a:[2],
 x:["A standard Lambda function cannot run longer than 15 minutes, so a 40-minute report cannot finish inside one synchronous call whatever the API's timeout is. The client would also have to hold a connection the whole time.",
    "An HTTP API has a maximum integration timeout of 30 seconds, which cannot be increased.",
    "The API call returns immediately, so nothing depends on a long-lived connection. A Standard workflow can run for up to a year, retries failed steps as defined, and its execution status can be read by a second API method.",
    "This depends on the client keeping a connection open for the whole build, which unreliable mobile networks do not allow, and it adds nothing that retries a failed step."],
 ref:[], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q13-013", day:13, dom:3, tags:["Step Functions"],
 q:"A fleet-telemetry platform receives about 3,000 device messages per second. Each message must go through the same four short steps: validate, enrich from a DynamoDB table, transform, and write to another table. The whole sequence finishes in under two seconds, every step is idempotent, and no execution history is needed beyond error logs in CloudWatch Logs. The team wants to define the sequence as a Step Functions state machine. Which configuration fits this workload?",
 o:["A Standard workflow that is started once for each message.",
    "An Express workflow that is started asynchronously for each message.",
    "A Standard workflow with a Distributed Map state that receives the device messages.",
    "An Express workflow that ends with a 10-minute Wait state to absorb late duplicates."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Standard workflows are meant for long-running, durable, auditable processes with exactly-once execution, and they are priced per state transition. None of that is needed for millions of two-second executions.",
    "Express workflows are designed for high-volume event processing such as IoT data ingestion. They run for up to five minutes, are billed by number of executions, duration and memory, and use an at-least-once model, which idempotent steps tolerate. Their history goes to CloudWatch Logs.",
    "A Distributed Map processes the items of a dataset, such as a list of S3 objects, as child executions of one run. A continuous flow of independent messages is not a dataset to iterate over.",
    "An Express workflow can run for at most five minutes, so a 10-minute Wait state cannot complete."],
 ref:[], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q13-014", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Step Functions"],
 q:"A media company uses a Step Functions Standard workflow to process uploaded films. One Task state sends a job to a rendering farm through an SQS queue with the <code>.waitForTaskToken</code> pattern; a render takes up to 3 hours. Twice last month a render node crashed without returning the token, and the executions stayed in the Running state for weeks until someone noticed. The company wants a crashed render to be detected within minutes and the job to be sent to the farm again automatically. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Set <code>HeartbeatSeconds</code> on the task and have the render nodes send a heartbeat with the task token every few minutes.",
    "Change the state machine to an Express workflow so that a stuck execution ends after five minutes.",
    "Add a Retry to the task that matches the <code>States.Timeout</code> error.",
    "Set only <code>TimeoutSeconds</code> to 4 hours on the task and add a Catch that ends the execution.",
    "Add a dead-letter queue to the SQS queue with a <code>maxReceiveCount</code> of 1."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Without a timeout a task waits for its token indefinitely. With a heartbeat interval, the task fails as soon as more than that interval passes without a heartbeat, so a crashed node is noticed within minutes even though a healthy render may take hours.",
    "Express workflows do not support the callback pattern and run for at most five minutes, so a three-hour render cannot be part of one.",
    "Step Functions reports <code>States.Timeout</code> when a task runs past its timeout or fails to send a heartbeat within the heartbeat interval. A retrier for that error runs the task again, which sends the job to the queue once more with a new token.",
    "This ends the wait, but only after four hours, and it stops the execution instead of sending the job again.",
    "A dead-letter queue sets aside messages that were received too often. It tells the state machine nothing, so the task would go on waiting for a token that never arrives."],
 ref:[], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q13-015", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Step Functions","Containers"],
 q:"A bank's nightly workflow in Step Functions must run a risk calculation that is packaged as a container. The calculation runs for 45 to 90 minutes as an Amazon ECS task on AWS Fargate, and the next state, which publishes the results, must start only after the task has finished successfully. If the task fails, the workflow must go to an alerting state. The team wants to write no polling code. Which design meets these requirements?",
 o:["A Standard workflow with a Task state that uses the ECS RunTask integration in its Run a Job (<code>.sync</code>) form and a Catch that leads to the alerting state.",
    "A Standard workflow with a Task state that uses the request-response ECS RunTask integration, followed directly by the publishing state.",
    "An Express workflow with a Task state that uses the ECS RunTask integration in its Run a Job (<code>.sync</code>) form to wait for the task.",
    "A Standard workflow with a Task state that invokes a Lambda function, which starts the task and loops until the task has stopped."],
 a:[0],
 x:["With the Run a Job pattern Step Functions starts the ECS task and waits until it has completed before it moves on. A failed task fails the state, and the Catch sends the execution to the alerting state. No polling code is needed.",
    "In the request-response pattern the workflow continues as soon as the RunTask call returns, so the results would be published while the calculation is still running.",
    "Express workflows support only request-response integrations and run for at most five minutes.",
    "This is polling code, and a Lambda invocation ends after 15 minutes, long before a 45-minute calculation."],
 ref:[], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q13-016", day:13, dom:3, tags:["Step Functions"],
 q:"An insurer's quote workflow in Step Functions calls three independent services for every request: a credit check, a vehicle-history lookup, and a fraud score. Today the three Task states run one after another and together take about nine seconds, although no call depends on another's result. The final pricing state needs all three results, and the quote must fail if any of the three calls still fails after its retries. The company wants to shorten the response time without merging the services. Which change meets these requirements?",
 o:["Put the three tasks in an Inline Map state with a maximum concurrency of three.",
    "Add a Choice state that sends each request to the service that usually answers first.",
    "Publish the request to an SNS topic that triggers the three services, then continue with the pricing state.",
    "Put the three tasks in the branches of a Parallel state that is followed by the pricing state."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A Map state runs one and the same set of steps for every item of a dataset. Three different service calls are three separate branches, which is what the Parallel state is for.",
    "A Choice state selects one path. The pricing state needs the results of all three services.",
    "The workflow would move on as soon as the message is published. It would not wait for the services or receive their results, so pricing would run without them.",
    "A Parallel state runs its branches concurrently and waits until all of them have finished; its output is an array with one result per branch. If any branch fails, the whole Parallel state fails."],
 ref:[], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q13-017", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Step Functions","Lambda"],
 q:"An online shop processes orders with five Lambda functions that call each other synchronously: validate, reserve stock, charge, create shipment, send confirmation. Each function waits for the next one to return, so the first function often reaches its timeout when a carrier responds slowly, the company pays for functions that are only waiting, and when a call fails nobody can tell which orders stopped at which step. The business also wants a separate path for orders above a certain value. The company wants to solve these problems with the LEAST custom code. Which solution should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Raise the timeout of each function to 15 minutes and configure provisioned concurrency on all five.",
    "Define the order flow as a Step Functions state machine that invokes each function as its own task.",
    "Have each function invoke the next one asynchronously and attach a dead-letter queue to every function.",
    "Merge the five functions into one function with more memory and a 15-minute timeout."],
 a:[1],
 x:["The functions would still wait for each other and be billed for it, and there would still be no view of where an order stopped.",
    "Step Functions calls the functions one after another and passes the data between them, so no function waits for another. Retries and error handling are declared per step, a Choice state adds the path for large orders, and each execution shows which step an order has reached.",
    "This ends the waiting, but the flow is now spread over five functions. Finding where an order stopped and adding the new branch remain custom code.",
    "A single function still runs into its timeout when a carrier is slow, and it gives no per-step retries or view of progress."],
 ref:[], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q13-018", day:13, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"An airline stores bookings in a DynamoDB table with a stream enabled. When a booking item is inserted with the status CONFIRMED, a Step Functions workflow must start, and it needs the passenger's loyalty tier, which is available only from an internal API on Amazon API Gateway. Updates and deletions, which make up 90% of the stream records, must be ignored. Today a Lambda function reads the stream, filters, calls the API and starts the workflow, and the team wants to retire this glue code. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST custom code?",
 o:["Create an EventBridge pipe with the stream as source, a filter for confirmed inserts, the API as enrichment, and the state machine as target.",
    "Create a rule on the default event bus that matches inserts into the table and has the state machine as its target.",
    "Keep the function and add filter criteria to its event source mapping so that it runs only for confirmed inserts.",
    "Subscribe an SNS topic to the stream, add a filter policy on the status, and subscribe the state machine to the topic."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A pipe connects one source to one target and can filter and enrich on the way. A DynamoDB stream is a supported source, API Gateway a supported enrichment and a Step Functions state machine a supported target, so the function is no longer needed. Only events that match the filter are charged.",
    "A rule acts on events that arrive on the bus and has no enrichment step. Something would still have to read the stream and fetch the loyalty tier.",
    "The filter saves invocations, but the code that calls the API and starts the workflow stays, and that is what the team wants to retire.",
    "A stream is read by polling it, not by subscribing a topic to it, and a state machine is not among the endpoint types that can subscribe to an SNS topic."],
 ref:[], doc:"pipes"},

{id:"q13-019", day:13, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"A subscription business publishes its own events (customer created, plan changed, subscription cancelled) to a custom EventBridge event bus. Marketing now wants every \"subscription cancelled\" event delivered to a third-party CRM, which accepts events only as HTTPS POST requests that carry an API key and allows at most 50 requests per second. Other event types must not be sent. The company wants no servers and no code to maintain, and delivery must be retried if the CRM is briefly unavailable. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a rule for the cancelled events that targets a Lambda function, which calls the CRM with a key from Secrets Manager.",
    "Create an EventBridge pipe with the event bus as source, a filter for cancelled events, and the CRM as target.",
    "Add a rule for the cancelled events that targets an SQS queue, and have the CRM poll the queue.",
    "Add a rule for the cancelled events that targets an API destination whose connection holds the API key and a rate limit."],
 a:[3],
 x:["This works, but the function is code to maintain, and it would have to implement the limit of 50 requests per second itself.",
    "An event bus is not a pipe source. Pipes read from DynamoDB streams, Kinesis streams, Amazon MQ brokers, Kafka streams and SQS queues.",
    "The CRM only accepts HTTPS POST requests. It cannot be made to poll an SQS queue.",
    "An API destination is an HTTPS endpoint that a rule can invoke like any other target. Its connection stores the authorization, the invocation rate can be limited per second, and EventBridge retries failed deliveries for up to 24 hours by default."],
 ref:[], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"q13-020", day:13, dom:3, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"A healthcare platform must send each patient a reminder 24 hours before an appointment, to within a minute or so. About two million appointments are booked each month, each at its own time and in its clinic's time zone, and appointments are often cancelled or rescheduled, in which case the pending reminder must be removed or replaced. The reminder itself is sent by an existing Lambda function. The company wants a serverless solution with minimal operational overhead and no process that scans a database for due reminders. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Send a message per appointment to an SQS queue with a delivery delay that ends 24 hours before the appointment.",
    "Create a one-time EventBridge Scheduler schedule per appointment that invokes the function and is deleted after it has run.",
    "Store an item per appointment in DynamoDB with a TTL at the reminder time, and send the reminder from the stream's delete record.",
    "Create an EventBridge scheduled rule per appointment on the default event bus with a cron expression for the reminder time."],
 a:[1],
 x:["An SQS delivery delay can be at most 15 minutes.",
    "EventBridge Scheduler is built for millions of schedules. A one-time schedule invokes its target once at a date and time in a chosen time zone, with 60-second precision, and can delete itself after completion. A cancelled appointment only needs its schedule deleted.",
    "DynamoDB deletes expired items within a few days of the expiry time, not at it, so the reminders would go out at unpredictable times.",
    "Scheduled rules are a legacy feature, they are evaluated in UTC only, and AWS recommends EventBridge Scheduler, which scales much further, for invoking targets on a schedule."],
 ref:[], doc:"scheduler"},

{id:"q13-021", day:13, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"An energy company routes meter-alarm events from a custom event bus to the billing team's REST API on Amazon API Gateway with a rule. The billing API was down for two days. When it came back, the events from the second day had never arrived and could not be found anywhere. The company requires that events that could not be delivered are kept, separately from the delivered ones, so that they can be processed once a target has recovered. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an archive on the event bus with indefinite retention and replay the period of an outage afterwards.",
    "Configure an SQS queue as the dead-letter queue of the rule's target.",
    "Change the rule's target to an SQS queue and write a Lambda function that forwards the messages to the API.",
    "Create an EventBridge global endpoint with an event bus in a second Region."],
 a:[1],
 x:["An archive stores the events of the bus whether or not they were delivered, and a replay resends everything in the chosen period. The billing API would receive again the events it had already processed.",
    "By default EventBridge retries a delivery for 24 hours and up to 185 times and then drops the event. With a dead-letter queue on the target, the events that still could not be delivered are stored in an SQS queue, which holds exactly the events that have to be processed later.",
    "The queue would hold the events, but the forwarding function is an extra component with code and error handling to maintain.",
    "A global endpoint routes incoming events to a bus in a secondary Region when the primary Region is unhealthy. Here the Region was fine and the target was down."],
 ref:[], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"q13-022", day:13, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge","S3 features"],
 q:"A media company's S3 bucket sends <code>ObjectCreated</code> event notifications for the <code>uploads/</code> prefix to a Lambda function that creates thumbnails. Two more consumers now need the same upload events: a Step Functions workflow that must start only for objects larger than 1 GB, and an SQS FIFO queue that feeds a billing service. The team's attempt to add more notification configurations for the same prefix and event type was rejected by Amazon S3. The company wants every consumer to receive the events it needs without adding relay functions. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Send the notifications to an SNS standard topic and subscribe the function, the state machine and the FIFO queue to it.",
    "Have the thumbnail function start the workflow and send the queue message after it has created each thumbnail.",
    "Turn on Amazon EventBridge for the bucket and create rules that target the function, the state machine and the FIFO queue.",
    "Replicate the uploads to two more buckets and configure one event notification on each of the three buckets."],
 a:[2],
 x:["A state machine is not an endpoint type that can subscribe to an SNS topic, so the workflow would still need a relay.",
    "This makes the thumbnail function a relay with extra code, and a failure in it would stop the other two consumers.",
    "With EventBridge turned on, S3 sends its events to the event bus, where any number of rules can match them. A rule can match on the object size with a numeric pattern, and state machines and SQS FIFO queues are rule targets. S3 itself cannot notify a FIFO queue or a state machine.",
    "This triples the storage, and S3 event notifications can still only go to SNS topics, SQS standard queues, Lambda functions or EventBridge."],
 ref:[], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"q13-023", day:13, dom:3, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A gaming company collects gameplay events from millions of devices in Amazon SQS for a fleet of consumers that aggregates statistics. At peak, producers send several hundred thousand messages per second. Each event carries its own timestamp and a unique ID, the consumers write with idempotent upserts keyed on that ID, and the order of arrival does not matter. An architect proposes a FIFO queue \"to be safe\". The company wants the simplest design that keeps up with the peak, without throughput quotas to plan for. Which solution should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["A FIFO queue with content-based deduplication and one message group ID for all events.",
    "A FIFO queue in high throughput mode with the device ID as the message group ID.",
    "A standard queue, relying on the consumers' idempotent writes.",
    "An Amazon MQ for RabbitMQ cluster sized for the peak message rate."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Without high throughput mode a FIFO queue handles 300 API calls per second per action, or 3,000 messages with batches of 10. A single message group is also processed strictly in order, so consumers could not work in parallel.",
    "High throughput mode raises the FIFO quotas, but they remain quotas that must be planned for, and the ordering and deduplication they pay for are not needed here.",
    "A standard queue supports a nearly unlimited number of API calls per second. Its trade-offs are at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering, and both are harmless when writes are idempotent and order does not matter.",
    "A broker runs on instances that must be sized and scaled for the peak. AWS recommends Amazon MQ for existing applications that depend on standard messaging protocols, and SQS for new ones."],
 ref:["d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q13-024", day:13, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A document-conversion service reads jobs from an SQS standard queue with workers on Amazon ECS. Most jobs finish in under a minute, but some large documents take up to 3 hours. The queue's visibility timeout was raised to 3 hours to stop long jobs from being processed twice. Since then, whenever a worker crashes, its job is not picked up again for up to 3 hours, and customers complain. The company wants jobs of crashed workers retried within a few minutes while a long job is still processed by only one worker at a time. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Keep the 3-hour visibility timeout and add a dead-letter queue with a <code>maxReceiveCount</code> of 1.",
    "Set a short visibility timeout and have each worker extend it regularly with <code>ChangeMessageVisibility</code> while it works.",
    "Set the visibility timeout to 0 and have each worker delete a message as soon as it has received it.",
    "Keep the 3-hour visibility timeout and set the queue's receive message wait time to 20 seconds."],
 a:[1],
 x:["The message of a crashed worker would still stay hidden for 3 hours, and it would then go to the dead-letter queue instead of to another worker.",
    "This is the heartbeat pattern. A message stays hidden only as long as its worker keeps extending the timeout, so the job of a crashed worker becomes visible again after one short timeout, while a long job stays invisible until it is done. The limit is 12 hours from the first receive.",
    "A job that is deleted on receipt is lost when its worker crashes, and with no visibility timeout two workers can receive the same message.",
    "Long polling reduces empty responses to receive calls. It has no effect on when an in-flight message becomes visible again."],
 ref:["d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q13-025", day:13, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"An insurer processes claims from an SQS standard queue that has a dead-letter queue. A faulty release rejected valid claims for two hours, and about 80,000 messages ended up in the dead-letter queue. The bug has been fixed and the consumers are healthy again. The operations team must get the stranded claims processed today while the consumers keep working on new claims from the source queue, without changing the consumers' configuration, without writing a script and without overloading the claims database, which tolerates only a limited write rate. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Raise <code>maxReceiveCount</code> in the source queue's redrive policy so that the messages return to it.",
    "Reconfigure the consumers to read from the dead-letter queue until it is empty, then switch them back.",
    "Increase the dead-letter queue's message retention period to 14 days so that the messages are delivered again.",
    "Start a dead-letter queue redrive to the source queue with a limit on the redrive velocity."],
 a:[3],
 x:["The redrive policy decides when a message is moved to the dead-letter queue. It never moves messages back.",
    "This changes the consumers' configuration twice, and new claims pile up in the source queue while the consumers work on the old ones.",
    "Retention only decides how long the messages are kept. Messages in a dead-letter queue go nowhere by themselves.",
    "A redrive moves the messages from the dead-letter queue back to the source queue without any code, and its velocity setting limits how many messages are moved per second, so the consumers and the database receive them at a rate they can handle."],
 ref:["d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q13-026", day:13, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A retailer is moving its warehouse system to AWS. Twenty existing services exchange messages through a self-managed RabbitMQ cluster by using AMQP 0-9-1 client libraries; their messaging code must not change, the operations team no longer wants to patch brokers, and messaging must keep working if one Availability Zone fails. At the same time a new team is building order-tracking services on AWS Lambda from scratch. It has no protocol requirements and wants messaging that needs no capacity planning. Which combination of solutions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["An Amazon MQ for RabbitMQ broker in a cluster deployment for the existing services.",
    "An Amazon MQ for RabbitMQ single-instance broker with an EBS volume for the existing services.",
    "Amazon SQS queues and Amazon SNS topics for the new services.",
    "A second Amazon MQ broker, sized for the expected peak, for the new services.",
    "A rewrite of the existing services to Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS before the migration."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Amazon MQ runs RabbitMQ as a managed service that existing AMQP 0-9-1 clients use by changing the endpoint. A cluster deployment has three broker nodes across Availability Zones, and AWS does the patching.",
    "A single-instance broker is one broker in one Availability Zone, so messaging stops if that zone fails.",
    "For applications built new in the cloud AWS recommends SQS and SNS: fully managed queues and topics with simple APIs that scale almost without limit, with no broker to size.",
    "A broker runs on instances whose size must be chosen for the load, which is the capacity planning the new team wants to avoid.",
    "SQS and SNS have their own APIs, so this means changing the messaging code of twenty services."],
 ref:[], doc:"mq"},

{id:"q13-027", day:13, dom:3, tags:["SNS"],
 q:"A payment processor's SaaS platform publishes transaction events as JSON documents to an SNS topic in a company's account. The platform cannot add message attributes; the event type and the amount exist only as fields inside the JSON body. Five SQS queues subscribe to the topic, and the chargeback team's queue should receive only events whose type is <code>chargeback</code>. Its subscription already has a filter policy on a <code>type</code> key, but the policy matches nothing and the queue stays empty. The company wants the queue to receive only chargeback events without adding components. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Turn on raw message delivery for the subscription so that the filter policy sees the JSON fields.",
    "Set the subscription's filter policy scope to <code>MessageBody</code> so that the policy is applied to the JSON fields.",
    "Remove the filter policy and have the chargeback consumer discard the events it does not need.",
    "Subscribe a Lambda function to the topic that copies chargeback events to the queue."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Raw message delivery removes the SNS metadata from the message that is delivered to the queue. It does not change what a filter policy is compared with.",
    "A filter policy is applied to message attributes by default, and these messages have none. With the scope set to <code>MessageBody</code>, SNS compares the policy with the properties of the JSON payload and delivers only the matching events.",
    "The queue would then receive every transaction event, which is what the company wants to avoid.",
    "This adds a component with code to maintain for something a subscription setting does."],
 ref:[], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q13-028", day:13, dom:1, tags:["SNS","SQS"],
 q:"A company publishes order events to an SNS standard topic. A new analytics team creates an SQS standard queue in the same account and Region and subscribes it to the topic through the API; the subscription is confirmed and has no filter policy. Messages published to the topic reach the other subscribers, but the new queue stays empty. The queue's access policy allows only the queue owner. The security team requires that nothing other than this topic can put messages in the queue. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Allow <code>sqs:SendMessage</code> on the queue in the IAM policy of the application that publishes to the topic.",
    "Add a statement to the topic's access policy that allows the queue to receive the topic's messages.",
    "Add a statement to the queue's access policy that allows the SNS service to send messages when the source ARN is the topic.",
    "Change the queue's access policy to allow <code>sqs:SendMessage</code> for all principals."],
 a:[2],
 x:["The publisher only calls SNS. The delivery to the queue is made by SNS, not with the publisher's permissions.",
    "The topic's policy controls who may publish to the topic or subscribe to it. The subscription already exists; the delivery is refused by the queue.",
    "A queue accepts messages from a topic only if the queue's policy allows the topic to perform <code>sqs:SendMessage</code>. A condition on <code>aws:SourceArn</code> limits this to the one topic.",
    "Deliveries would start, but any principal could then send messages to the queue, which breaks the security requirement."],
 ref:[], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q13-029", day:13, dom:2, tags:["SNS"],
 q:"A logistics company publishes shipment events to an SNS standard topic that has eight subscribers. Auditors now require that every published message is also stored in Amazon S3 for seven years, where analysts can query it later. The company wants no code to maintain, must not change the publishers, and must not affect the existing subscribers. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Turn on message archiving for the topic with a retention period of seven years.",
    "Subscribe a Lambda function to the topic that writes each message to the bucket as an object.",
    "Subscribe an SQS queue to the topic and run a consumer on EC2 that writes batches to the bucket.",
    "Subscribe an Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream with an S3 destination to the topic."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Message archiving exists only for FIFO topics, and it keeps messages for at most 365 days inside SNS, not in S3.",
    "This works, but the function is code to maintain.",
    "This adds a server to run and patch and a consumer to maintain.",
    "A Firehose delivery stream can subscribe to a topic like any other endpoint and deliver the messages to an S3 bucket. AWS describes this as the way to keep a topic's messages for compliance or archival, and it needs no code and no change to publishers or other subscribers."],
 ref:[], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q13-030", day:13, dom:1, tags:["Cognito"],
 q:"A museum's mobile app shows exhibit photos stored in a private S3 bucket and lets signed-in members save favourites to a DynamoDB table. Visitors must be able to browse the photos before they create an account; members sign in through an existing Amazon Cognito user pool. The app calls S3 and DynamoDB directly with the AWS SDK, no AWS credentials may be shipped inside the app, and guests must get read access to the photos and nothing else. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a shared guest user to the user pool, embed its password in the app, and call S3 and DynamoDB with the user pool tokens.",
    "Create a Cognito identity pool with guest access, a role for unauthenticated identities that can only read the photos, and a role for members.",
    "Make the photo prefix public with a bucket policy and call DynamoDB with the members' user pool tokens.",
    "Create an IAM user that can only read the photos and store its access key in the app's configuration for guests."],
 a:[1],
 x:["A password inside the app is a shipped credential. User pool tokens are also not AWS credentials: a user pool authenticates users and issues JSON web tokens.",
    "An identity pool exchanges identities for temporary AWS credentials and can also issue narrowly scoped credentials to guests who have not signed in. Members who sign in through the user pool receive the credentials of the authenticated role instead.",
    "The bucket must stay private, and a user pool token cannot sign DynamoDB requests. AWS credentials for members come from an identity pool.",
    "An access key in the app's configuration is a long-term credential shipped with the app."],
 ref:["d8c1"], doc:"cognito"},

{id:"q13-031", day:13, dom:1, tags:["Cognito","API Gateway"],
 q:"A company exposes a REST API on API Gateway to partner backend systems that run unattended on the partners' servers. Reporting partners may only call the GET methods; fulfilment partners may also call <code>POST /shipments</code>. The company already uses an Amazon Cognito user pool and wants standard OAuth 2.0 access tokens, no custom authorizer code, and API Gateway itself to reject a call when the token does not permit that method. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create one user pool user per partner and validate the user's ID token with a Cognito authorizer on every method.",
    "Create a Cognito identity pool and have all partners sign requests with temporary credentials of one shared IAM role.",
    "Give each partner an API key in a usage plan, and list in the plan the methods that the partner is allowed to call.",
    "Define custom scopes in the user pool, give each partner an app client for the client credentials grant, and require a scope on each method."],
 a:[3],
 x:["An ID token proves who signed in. Without scopes every valid token passes every method, so the two kinds of partner could not be told apart, and unattended systems would have to hold user passwords.",
    "These are AWS credentials, not OAuth 2.0 tokens, and one shared role gives both kinds of partner the same permissions.",
    "AWS says not to use API keys for authentication or authorization. They identify clients for throttling and quotas.",
    "This is machine-to-machine authorization: an app client with a secret obtains an access token that contains only the custom scopes it was granted. A Cognito authorizer with authorization scopes on a method accepts access tokens and checks them for an accepted scope."],
 ref:[], doc:"cognito"},

{id:"q13-032", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A company with eight developers is splitting a monolith into a dozen containerized services that will run on AWS only. Nobody on the team has operated Kubernetes, there are no Kubernetes manifests or tools to keep, and there is no plan to run the workload anywhere else. The team wants an IAM role per service, Application Load Balancers and CloudWatch to work out of the box, no orchestrator version upgrades to plan, and no servers to patch. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Amazon EKS with managed node groups.",
    "Amazon EKS with AWS Fargate.",
    "Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate.",
    "Amazon ECS with EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Kubernetes releases new versions several times a year and retires old ones, so cluster upgrades have to be planned, and the nodes are EC2 instances. The team would also have to learn Kubernetes without needing its portability.",
    "Fargate removes the nodes, but the cluster is still Kubernetes, with versions to upgrade and skills the team does not have.",
    "Amazon ECS is AWS's own orchestrator, fully managed and versionless, with AWS integrations built in. On Fargate there are no instances to provision, patch or scale.",
    "ECS fits, but with EC2 instances the team has to patch and scale the hosts."],
 ref:[], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"q13-033", day:13, dom:3, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A company runs its video-analysis pipeline on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate. A new inference service needs an NVIDIA GPU in every task. The other services must stay on Fargate, and the team wants to keep one cluster and the same deployment pipeline for everything. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a GPU requirement to the inference service's Fargate task definition.",
    "Give the inference service's Fargate tasks the largest size, 16 vCPU and 120 GB of memory.",
    "Move all services to a new Amazon EKS cluster with a GPU node group.",
    "Add an Auto Scaling group of GPU instances to the cluster as a capacity provider for the inference service."],
 a:[3],
 x:["The <code>gpu</code> parameter of a task definition is not valid for Fargate tasks.",
    "More vCPUs and memory are not a GPU, and the service requires one.",
    "This would give the service GPUs, but it moves every service off ECS on Fargate and replaces the cluster and the pipeline, which the team wants to keep.",
    "ECS runs GPU workloads on container instances of GPU instance types. A cluster can combine capacity providers, so the inference service uses the GPU instances while the other services keep using Fargate in the same cluster."],
 ref:[], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"q13-034", day:13, dom:1, tags:["Containers","IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A team deploys a new service on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate. The task definition pulls its image from a private Amazon ECR repository, injects a database password from AWS Secrets Manager as an environment variable, and sends logs with the <code>awslogs</code> driver. The application code itself only reads and writes objects in one S3 bucket. Tasks fail to start with an error that the secret cannot be retrieved. The task role allows the S3 actions and <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on the secret; the task execution role has only the permissions for ECR and CloudWatch Logs. What should a solutions architect do to fix the problem while keeping least privilege?",
 o:["Grant <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on the secret to the task execution role and remove it from the task role.",
    "Attach the <code>AdministratorAccess</code> managed policy to the task role until the tasks start.",
    "Grant <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on the secret to the role of the pipeline that deploys the service.",
    "Build the password into the container image and remove the secret reference from the task definition."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Secrets referenced in a task definition are fetched by the ECS agent before the container starts, with the task execution role, which is also the role that pulls the image and writes the logs. The task role is for the application's own calls, here only S3, so it no longer needs the permission.",
    "The agent does not use the task role, so the tasks would still fail, and administrator access is the opposite of least privilege.",
    "The pipeline's role is used to register task definitions and update the service. It is not used when a task starts.",
    "The tasks would start, but anyone who can pull the image could read the password, and changing it would mean a new image."],
 ref:[], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"q13-035", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A company stores its container images in Amazon ECR private repositories in eu-west-1 and runs them on Amazon ECS. Two requirements are new. First, images that are already in the repositories must be checked again when new vulnerabilities are published, for operating system packages and for the application's programming-language libraries. Second, a disaster recovery environment in eu-central-1 must be able to pull every image that is pushed from now on even if eu-west-1 is unavailable. Which combination of actions meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Turn on basic scanning with scan on push for every repository.",
    "Turn on enhanced scanning with continuous scanning for the repositories.",
    "Add a pipeline step that also pushes every image to a registry in eu-central-1.",
    "Add a cross-Region replication rule for eu-central-1 to the registry's settings.",
    "Create ECR interface VPC endpoints in eu-central-1 for the eu-west-1 registry."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Basic scanning covers operating system vulnerabilities only and runs on push or on request. An image that is already stored is not checked again when a new vulnerability is published.",
    "Enhanced scanning uses Amazon Inspector to scan for operating system and programming-language package vulnerabilities, and with continuous scanning the findings are updated as new vulnerabilities appear.",
    "This would put the images in the second Region, but it is one more step to maintain in every pipeline for something the registry can do itself.",
    "A private registry can replicate its repositories to other Regions. Images pushed after the rule is created are then also stored in eu-central-1 and can be pulled there without eu-west-1; content that existed before is not replicated.",
    "An endpoint is only a private path to a registry. The images would still exist in eu-west-1 alone."],
 ref:[], doc:"ecr"},

{id:"q13-036", day:13, dom:3, tags:["Containers","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An Amazon ECS service runs on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group with a fixed size of six. Service auto scaling with a target tracking policy raises the desired number of tasks during peaks, but the additional tasks cannot be placed because the six instances have no unreserved CPU or memory left. The company must stay on EC2 instances because of software that is licensed per host. It wants the number of instances to follow what the tasks need, upward and downward, without writing scaling logic. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Lower the target value of the service's target tracking policy so that it scales out earlier.",
    "Add a target tracking policy on the average CPU utilization of the instances to the Auto Scaling group.",
    "Create an Auto Scaling group capacity provider with managed scaling and use it for the service.",
    "Add scheduled actions to the Auto Scaling group that set its size to twelve during business hours."],
 a:[2],
 x:["The service would ask for more tasks sooner, and they would still find no room on the six instances.",
    "This follows what the instances use, not what the tasks reserve. Instances can be fully reserved while their CPU use is low, so tasks would still wait.",
    "With managed scaling ECS creates and manages a target tracking policy for the Auto Scaling group that is driven by the capacity the tasks need, so instances are added when tasks cannot be placed and removed when they are no longer needed.",
    "A schedule follows the clock, not the demand. It leaves instances idle on quiet days and falls short on peaks above twelve."],
 ref:[], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"q13-037", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A manufacturer runs data-processing containers on Amazon ECS in AWS. A new plant produces machine data that, by contract, must be processed on servers inside the plant; the results are then sent to an API in AWS. The plant already has suitable Linux servers with an outbound internet connection, and the company will not buy new hardware. The platform team wants to deploy and monitor the plant's containers with the same ECS task definitions, API and console that it uses in the cloud, without operating an orchestrator itself. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Install Amazon EKS Anywhere on the plant's servers and convert the task definitions to Kubernetes manifests.",
    "Order an AWS Outposts rack for the plant and run the ECS tasks on it.",
    "Run the tasks on AWS Fargate in the nearest Region and send the machine data to them over a VPN.",
    "Register the plant's servers as external instances with Amazon ECS Anywhere and run the tasks on them."],
 a:[3],
 x:["EKS Anywhere is a customer-managed product: the company would operate Kubernetes clusters itself and would have to give up its ECS task definitions.",
    "An Outpost is AWS-managed hardware that is installed on site, so it is new hardware, which the company has ruled out.",
    "The processing would then take place in an AWS Region, not inside the plant.",
    "ECS Anywhere registers on-premises servers or virtual machines with an ECS cluster as external instances, and tasks run on them with the EXTERNAL launch type. The orchestrator remains the ECS service in AWS. External instances suit workloads that process data and send traffic outward, as here."],
 ref:[], doc:"ecs-anywhere"},

{id:"q13-038", day:13, dom:2, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A defence contractor runs its applications on Amazon EKS in AWS and must now run the same Kubernetes workloads in a secure facility whose network has no connection to the internet or to any AWS Region. The facility has its own VMware vSphere servers. The contractor wants Kubernetes that is consistent with Amazon EKS, with tooling for creating and upgrading clusters, and accepts that its own staff will operate the clusters. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Attach the facility's servers to the existing cluster as Amazon EKS Hybrid Nodes.",
    "Deploy Amazon EKS Anywhere clusters on the facility's servers.",
    "Register the facility's servers with Amazon ECS Anywhere and run the workloads as ECS tasks.",
    "Install an AWS Outposts rack in the facility and run Amazon EKS on AWS Outposts."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Hybrid nodes are worker nodes on your own machines for a cluster whose control plane runs in an AWS Region, and they need a reliable connection to that Region.",
    "EKS Anywhere automates creating and operating Kubernetes clusters on your own infrastructure, including VMware vSphere, and it can run disconnected from AWS in air-gapped environments. The clusters are customer-managed, which the contractor accepts.",
    "ECS is not Kubernetes, so the workloads would have to be rewritten as ECS tasks, and external instances are managed by the ECS service in an AWS Region.",
    "Amazon EKS on AWS Outposts also requires a reliable connection from the site to an AWS Region."],
 ref:[], doc:"eks-anywhere"},

{id:"q13-039", day:13, dom:2, tags:["CloudWatch","Step Functions"],
 q:"A ticketing application handles each purchase through an API Gateway REST API, three Lambda functions, a Step Functions workflow and two DynamoDB tables. About 2% of purchases take more than eight seconds, but the CloudWatch metrics of each service look normal and the logs of each function show nothing unusual. The team needs to follow individual slow requests from the API through every downstream call and see which component adds the delay, with as little code change as possible. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable AWS X-Ray tracing on the API stage, the functions and the state machine, and examine the slow traces.",
    "Build a CloudWatch dashboard that shows the p99 latency metrics of all the services next to each other.",
    "Turn on AWS CloudTrail data events for Lambda and DynamoDB and query them with Amazon Athena.",
    "Run CloudWatch Logs Insights queries that sort the log events of each function by duration."],
 a:[0],
 x:["X-Ray records each traced request as it passes through the integrated services and shows the time spent in every downstream call. API Gateway REST APIs, Lambda and Step Functions send trace data once tracing is turned on, and the trace map shows where latency builds up.",
    "Metrics are aggregated per service. They cannot show the path of one request or which hop made that request slow.",
    "CloudTrail records API activity for auditing. It does not link the calls of one purchase into a timeline of latencies.",
    "This shows how long each function ran, one function at a time. It does not connect the calls that belong to one purchase or cover the API, the workflow and the tables."],
 ref:[], doc:"xray"},

{id:"q13-040", day:13, dom:3, tags:["CloudWatch","Lambda"],
 q:"A serverless application consists of 40 Lambda functions, each writing JSON logs to its own CloudWatch Logs log group. Two requirements are new. A few times a month, engineers must find every log event with a given order ID across all functions in the last 24 hours and count errors per function, within minutes and with nothing to run between investigations. In addition, every log event with the level ERROR must reach the security team's Amazon Kinesis data stream within a few minutes of being written. The functions' logging must not change. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create an export task to Amazon S3 whenever a complaint arrives and query the exported files with Amazon Athena.",
    "Run CloudWatch Logs Insights queries across the functions' log groups for the investigations.",
    "Create a metric filter for the level ERROR on each log group and a CloudWatch alarm on the metric.",
    "Create subscription filters with a pattern for the level ERROR that deliver to the Kinesis data stream.",
    "Schedule a Lambda function every minute that runs a Logs Insights query and copies the results to the stream."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Log data can take up to 12 hours to become available for export, so answers would not come within minutes.",
    "Logs Insights searches and aggregates log data interactively over many log groups, with nothing to provision between investigations.",
    "A metric filter turns matching events into a number for a metric. It does not deliver the events themselves, and it does not apply to events that were ingested before it existed.",
    "A subscription filter delivers the log events that match its pattern to a Kinesis data stream, a Firehose stream or a Lambda function soon after ingestion, usually within three minutes.",
    "This is polling code to write and run. Subscription filters provide the feed as a managed feature."],
 ref:[], doc:"logs-insights"}
);
